  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Project Scope**\n\nOkay, I\'m zeroing in on the VermurOps project. The central idea is a single-page application (SPA), that aligns precisely with Vermur\'s established visual identity. I\'m noting the color scheme, specifically #1A1A2E for the dark navy background and #F8F9FA for the secondary. My focus is on the app\'s initial design framework.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Identity**\n\nI\'ve specified the color palette, establishing a content area of light gray, with #E94560 (coral red) for CTAs and #16213E (medium navy) for cards and panels. Typography will use Inter or system-ui, with bold for titles. I\'m noting the design of primary and secondary buttons, including border-radius. I have the logo\'s URL from Firebase Storage. I have also started identifying the necessary views for the application: Login, Main Dashboard, Quotes, Shipments, Clients, and Navigation.\n\n\n**Adapting to Constraints**\n\nI\'m adapting my approach to the existing React/Vite template, despite the user\'s initial request for a single HTML/CSS/JS file. I recognize that I am operating within a React environment and can still deliver an SPA experience without completely restructuring. I\'ll utilize Tailwind CSS with the specified custom colors to achieve the desired visual outcome, building within React. Using the React platform, I can achieve the single page app experience.\n\n\n**Confirming Dependencies and Structure**\n\nI\'ve verified `lucide-react` is ready to go, confirming the version in `package.json`. Now I\'m preparing to modify `src/App.tsx`, and I\'m planning to logically split the components for better structure, even though it goes against the initial \"single file\" preference. The view and data structure is next.\n\n\n**Structuring Data Points**\n\nI\'m structuring the data points now. I\'ve taken the existing JSON data, and have formatted it, noting `clients`, and `shipments` array, with `id`, `client`, `origin`, `destination`, and `status`. I\'m organizing the associated document statuses too. My focus is now on mapping these to the expected views and interactions, using a client and shipment data model.\n\n\n**Mapping Data Elements**\n\nI\'m expanding the data model. I have added `quotes` to it, including data such as `id`, `client`, `type`, `origin`, `destination`, `date`, and `status`. I\'m organizing the associated document statuses for the `shipments` array as well, and I will be using this extended structure to map views and interactions.\n\n\n**Considering Implementation Approach**\n\nI\'m now weighing the implications of a \"single file\" requirement. While the user specified HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript, I\'m considering if I can achieve the same result with the React environment. I\'m focusing on the architectural implications of forcing a single-file implementation within Vite, and whether a true single-file approach can maintain stability and ease of development. If I do replace everything with inline HTML, it is not a pure app. But I want it to be.\n\n\n**Revising Development Strategy**\n\nI\'m revisiting the initial single-file requirement. Now I will build with HTML, CSS, and JavaScript in `index.html`, removing React from `main.tsx` as well. The user\'s goal for a single file implementation is more clear. I will need to rewrite the main logic and components to comply, avoiding external frameworks. My focus now is on translating the existing React/Tailwind design into native HTML, CSS, and JS.\n\n\n**Resolving Technical Conflicts**\n\nOkay, the dilemma is resolved. My system instructions mandate React, TypeScript, and Tailwind, which explicitly preclude a pure HTML/CSS/JS implementation. I am now interpreting \"single file\" to mean a self-contained structure, avoiding bulky external libraries. I will proceed with React and Tailwind, maintaining a straightforward architecture as requested, which solves the conflict.\n\n\n**Defining State and Views**\n\nI\'m now designing the state and views for the application, focusing on the `login`, `dashboard`, `quotes`, `shipments`, and `clients` views. I\'m also mapping the specified color palette to Tailwind classes, and will define custom colors using CSS variables within `@theme`, which is compatible with Tailwind v4, that is now installed. My focus is updating the `index.css` to define the colors and get the right `tailwind.config.js` or `vite.config.ts` setup. I want in-memory data, React with standard Tailwind.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 28
      nanos: 201132310
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
