    thinking: "**Defining the Aesthetic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on translating the \"Quiet Luxury / Linear / Stripe style\" brief into concrete UI elements for the React/Tailwind VermurOps application. The B2B SaaS context is key. I\'m starting with the base dark mode color for the sidebar and topbar, which will be #0A0A0C. Next, I will flesh out the content canvas.\n\n\n**Refining the Color Palette**\n\nI\'ve fleshed out the visual details, incorporating #FAFAFB for the canvas, and #E11D48 (Vermur Red) as the primary accent. I\'m focusing on KPI cards, tables, typography (Inter with tabular numbers), badge styles (tinted backgrounds), and button styling with 8px radius and primary red. Now I will build the initial setup of Tailwind and React to implement these features step by step.\n\n\n**Implementing the Dark Palette**\n\nI\'m now integrating the dark theme colors into the Tailwind configuration. I\'ve defined `--color-dark-base`, `--color-dark-surface`, and `--color-dark-border` in `index.css`. This is alongside existing color definitions. Next, I will be updating the components in the modules, specifically cards, tables, badges, headers, and spacing, to reflect the design system.\n\n\n**Defining the Theme Variables**\n\nI\'ve defined the complete set of CSS variables for the light theme, including fonts, brand colors, surfaces, text, and states like success, warning, info, and danger. I also added a `tabular-nums` class for the Inter font, which sets the `font-variant-numeric` property to `tabular-nums` for tabular numbers. The base style now applies `--color-canvas` and `--color-text-primary` to the `body` element.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 16
      nanos: 597236143
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
