  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the Portal**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the \"Portal del Cliente,\" the digital freight portal\'s external interface. The Vermur branding and client login are prioritized for the top bar. The \"Rastrea tu embarque\" section with the tracking number input and timeline display is taking shape, and is core to the experience.\n\n\n**Developing Client Portal Features**\n\nI\'m now integrating \"Mis embarques,\" \"Solicita una cotizaci\303\263n,\" \"Mis facturas,\" and \"Mi inventario en almac\303\251n\" into the client portal. I\'m exploring how to best integrate this external portal into the existing application; either as a standalone view, or a component. A new sidebar option for demonstration, labeled \"Portal de Cliente (Ejemplo)\" seems like the best approach. I\'m now planning the layout in `ClientPortal.tsx`, considering a completely distinct interface from the internal dashboard.\n\n\n**Refining External Portal Integration**\n\nI\'m now contemplating how to best integrate the client portal. Previously, I was considering rendering `ClientPortal` as a distinct view alongside the internal dashboard. I\'m now leaning towards making the `ClientPortal` component the exclusive content when the `currentView` is set to \'portal\'. For the demo, I\'ll keep the sidebar. However, the ideal approach for a production environment would be to render it outside the admin frame entirely.\n\n\n**Implementing Portal Access**\n\nI\'ve made significant progress implementing the client portal access. Now, the `App.tsx` file conditionally renders the client portal based on `currentView`. I added a \"Ver Portal de Cliente\" button in the `Header` component. The `ClientPortal.tsx` component is starting to take shape, with the Vermur logo, navigation, user profile, and a prominent \"Rastrea tu embarque\" search input.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 71
      nanos: 204570618
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
